Unreleased
- New feature: Multisync's cross-provider id atlas gains a second source, the community arm database (SlashNephy/arm-supplementary), which supplies the Annict ids anime-relations has no column for. This is the only way an Annict entry can be linked at all -- Annict's titles are Japanese-only, so the "exactly one matching title" step that normally rescues an unlinked entry never fires for it, and it publishes no AniList id of its own. The two sources are additive: an arm row tying an Annict work to a MyAnimeList id joins up with an anime-relations rule for that same id, so an Annict entry reaches Kitsu through a provider neither database named directly. Measured over 400 works, arm's MyAnimeList ids agreed with Annict's own on all 271 where both had one, with no disagreements. The file is fetched at runtime and cached alongside the redirections database rather than being shipped in the tree (its upstream carries no license); it refreshes weekly, only rows naming Annict are ingested so existing MAL/Kitsu/AniList linking is untouched, and setting "arm_time" to 0 in config.json turns it off entirely.
- New feature: Annict (annict.com) is supported as an account type, over its GraphQL API. Anime only. Sign in with a read/write personal access token created at annict.com/settings/apps, pasted as the PIN like any other OAuth backend. Annict has no episodes-watched counter -- watching an episode creates a Record object and the only readable per-episode fact is a boolean -- so progress is translated in both directions: reading it takes the contiguous run of watched episodes from the first (never the highest watched episode, which would make the next update fill in every gap below it with new records on your public activity feed), and a show marked Completed with no episode records at all reports as fully watched rather than as 0 of N. Advancing progress creates only the records that are actually missing and never shares them to Twitter/Facebook -- and since every record is a post on your public activity feed, a jump that would manufacture a whole series' worth at once (adding an already-finished show through multisync, say) sets the status and says so instead of quietly posting fifty times; creating an already-finished entry likewise just marks it Completed, which reads back as fully watched anyway. Lowering progress deletes the records above the new position where they can still be found (Annict offers no way to look up your own record for a given episode, so that search is a bounded scan of recent history and says so when it comes up empty). Removing a show clears its status to "no state" and leaves your records alone, since Annict has no real delete. Movies (and shorts) register no episodes on Annict at all, so there is nothing to record against -- those count as a single episode, watched or not according to the status, the way every other backend already models a film. Cover art is a special case: Annict hosts its own posters but exposes none of them through the API -- the one field that would return them is gated on the caller being an Annict administrator, and the three image URLs it does hand out are scraped from the anime's social media (Annict picks whichever of the Facebook card, the Twitter card and the Twitter profile avatar has the most pixels), which is why they so rarely look like the show. Annict's own site never displays those either. So neither does this: covers are read from the work's page, the same artwork the site shows, cached permanently on disk and filled in twenty at a time per sync so a large library doesn't turn one sync into hundreds of page loads. Two known limits, both the API's: there are no per-show scores (its only rating lives on an individual episode record), and keyword search matches the Japanese title only -- it will not find a romaji or English name. Local file matching is unaffected, since English/kana titles are carried as aliases.
- Bugfix: Multisync no longer manufactures a conflict when sides actually agree and one provider simply has no value: with manual reconciliation, an empty side (no finish date on MAL, an unrated score) abstains instead of voting, so a value three sides hold unanimously is adopted -- and filled in on the empty side -- without asking. An empty value only competes when history proves it was deliberately cleared since the last sync, in which case the clear propagates like any other single-sided change (and choosing an empty side in a conflict is now labeled "clears it everywhere").
- Bugfix: Resolving a multisync conflict now actually produces the follow-up sync instead of dead-ending or re-raising the same conflict. Two causes fixed: the planner judged whether local state had moved by "equals some provider's base", which misread a just-adopted resolution as "local never changed" (local's stored provenance -- a provider echo vs a deliberate edit or resolution -- now decides which comparison applies), and resolution only advanced the bases of the providers listed in the conflict, so a provider the precision collapse had folded away (MAL's integer 8 under a finer 8.4) kept re-conflicting forever (every mapped provider's current value is now acknowledged). Every resolution choice -- keep Hakubun, use a provider, an explicit value, an explicit clear -- is regression-tested through resolve -> replan -> apply -> provider state.
- Improvement: Multisync now treats "should this entry exist on this provider" as its own concept with real provenance, separate from field ownership. A creation is proposed only when actual provider entries establish the work's existence, and every proposal says so ("exists on AniList and Kitsu; Mal has no entry") instead of the meaningless "from Hakubun" -- an entity no provider lists (local-only) is never offered for creation at all. Declining is now durable and distinct from unticking: right-click a proposed new entry to never be asked about that title on that site again, recorded alongside the existing "user deleted it on the website" and "cross-id lookup found nothing" exclusions (each remembered with its reason), none of which are ever re-proposed on a later fetch. Provider-only remains what it says -- the entity is isolated from cross-provider sync -- without implying anything about field authority.
- Improvement: The Multi-provider Sync window (Qt and GTK) is reorganized around what the user needs to know instead of the engine's internals, with both toolkits now sharing one information architecture and vocabulary. The app's own reconciled state is labeled "Hakubun" everywhere -- never "local" and never the signed-in provider (the signed-in account is shown separately and carries no sync authority). The Sync tab leads with a headline of the three numbers that matter ("3 change(s) · 1 decision(s) needed · 2 new entries"); new-entry creations get their own list beside Changes (still unticked until you opt in) instead of hiding as amber rows in the ordinary change list; conflicts appear as "Decisions" cards that list what Hakubun and each site hold and say in plain language why an automatic choice wasn't possible. The ownership matrix is replaced as the everyday configuration surface by a per-field rule picker in consequences ("Keep the furthest progress", "Keep from Mal", "Ask me when they differ", "Don't sync"); the full matrix survives in the new Advanced tab -- kept in agreement with the simple view in both directions -- alongside the identity Inspector and the destructive Reset, which no longer sits next to the Sync button. Buttons and messages now describe outcomes ("Fetch changes", "Sync selected", "Found 3 change(s), 1 decision(s) needed") rather than engine terminology ("Fetch & Plan", "Apply", "Planned: ... conflict(s)"), and the Identity tab leads with "N title(s) need matching". No sync behavior changed: selection checkboxes, opt-in creates, structural episode resolution, cancellation, incremental replanning with preserved ticks (Qt), failure isolation and the identity workflow all work exactly as before.
- Rework: Multisync is rebuilt around field strategies, replacing the git-like three-way merge engine. Each field now follows one explicit policy -- owned by a provider (that tracker's value is authoritative everywhere, always: an edit on its website propagates with no conflict, an in-app edit is routed to it first, and a non-owner's edit converges back), individual (never synchronized, never compared), or reconciled by a strategy (Manual, Union, Highest, Lowest, or the progress-aware strategy that honours a single-sided rewatch reset and otherwise takes the furthest episode). The Mirror/Pull/Merge/Rebase mode dropdown is gone from the sync window and Settings -- the ownership matrix is the only steering wheel -- and every planned change and conflict now names the policy or strategy that produced it, in the preview and in the Inspector's new per-field Policy readout. The old NO_BASE/IN_SYNC/PULL/PUSH/BOTH divergence classifier, its generic resolver, and the "first sync" coin-flip rows are all gone; per-provider sync history (the merge base) survives as data the strategies consult to tell which side actually changed. Rebase's add-missing-entries feature now rides along in every plan as unticked opt-in rows, and an entry you deleted on a tracker's website is never offered for re-creation. Existing ownership settings migrate automatically (Local/Ask become Manual reconciliation, Merge becomes Union). See docs/multisync.md.
- Bugfix: Adding, removing, or retitling a show on your list -- with no local file ever touched -- didn't reliably show up in the local library, and switching between accounts on different sites made this dramatically worse ("found" under one account, "not found" or matched to the wrong show under another with the exact same files on disk). The local-library scan-skip check only fingerprinted the search directories' mtimes, so it had no way to notice that the tracker list itself had changed since the last real scan; a file whose title didn't match anything at scan time stayed permanently unmatched even after the show was later added to the list, since nothing on disk ever moved to trigger a rescan. The fingerprint now also covers every in-scope show's id, title, and aliases, and a change there forces every file to be freshly re-matched (not just files under a changed directory), so the local library can no longer drift out of sync with what your list actually says.
- Bugfix: Library scanning was missing far more shows than it should for anyone with a large "Completed"/"Dropped" list -- not a regression, but every parser wrapper (AnimeInfoExtractor, Anitopy, Anitomy-NG) unconditionally appends " Season N" (plus type/year) to a parsed title, which routinely dragged an otherwise-correct match below guess_show's fuzzy cutoff for any franchise the tracker holds as one continuous entry (Naruto, Bleach, One Punch Man, etc. -- confirmed against a real ~10k-file/~700-show library: this alone explained roughly 40% of otherwise-unexplained scan misses). guess_show now retries once with that decoration stripped whenever the decorated title fails to match, only ever recovering a match the undecorated title would have won on its own -- it can't steal a match from a title that already matched, or invent one for an unrelated show.

v0.13
- New feature: An airing show's details now say when its next episode is due -- "Next episode will air in: 3 days (Sat Aug 02, 2026)" -- reduced to days, hours or minutes exactly as the airing scheduler does, and anchored to the absolute date a lone row needs. Resolved from the list itself on AniList accounts, and cross-referenced from AniList by MAL id on every other backend (cached for an hour, and shared with the airing schedule so viewing one makes the other free).
- New feature: A show pinned to a folder with "Set folder" now says so in its details (Qt and GTK), and in Taiga mode also on the Edit tab next to the controls that change it. Previously nothing anywhere showed which folder a show was pinned to, or that it was pinned at all.
- Improvement: Library scanning is dramatically faster. Fuzzy-matching parsed filenames against the tracked list dominated scan time -- on a 684-show list (4,932 titles and aliases) it was ~58ms per unique title, minutes of a full scan. It now uses rapidfuzz when installed (the new `fuzzy` extra), which is over 400x faster; without it, the difflib sweep skips comparisons its own cheap upper bounds already rule out, for roughly 3x. Both produce the same matches as before.
- Bugfix: (anitomy-ng parser) A filename like "[HnY] Beyblade X 11 - Kadovar's Test (1080p) v2.mkv" -- an episode number with no dash in front of it, followed by an episode title -- was parsed with the number and the episode title swallowed into the show title and no episode detected at all, so the file was silently recorded as episode 1. The wrapper now recovers the episode number from such a title, leaving the shapes anitomy-ng already handles ("Title - 11 - Name", a bare trailing "Title 11") untouched and refusing to read a four-digit year as an episode.
- New feature: Multisync -- a local-first, reconciliation-based multi-provider synchronization subsystem (Qt: List -> Multi-provider Sync, or Tools in Taiga mode; GTK: its own Multi-sync window, matching the Qt feature set). Internal UUID identity with provider-ID mappings, exact-ID automatic linking, a user-confirmed identity workflow (confirm / keep provider-only / defer / ignore), three-way diffs against a per-provider merge base, per-field ownership (Local / a provider / Merge / Individual / Ask) edited from the sync window's ownership matrix, sync preview with skip/resolve, an append-only event log with undo and watch/rating history, mirror/pull/rebase/merge modes, per-provider failure isolation, and score-scale conversion with rounding. See docs/multisync.md.
- New feature: (GTK) A full Multi-sync window (Tier 3), reaching parity with Qt: preview, ownership matrix, identity workflow, and an Identity Inspector, plus a local-agnostic list that rates an owned Score in the owner's own system instead of the signed-in account's.
- New feature: Anime and manga are synced through entirely separate multisync databases -- a field ownership or identity decision on one media type can never leak into or conflict with the other, and a sync run always covers every connected provider for the media type being synced (previously a provider without a library entry loaded yet for that type could be silently skipped, e.g. Kitsu).
- New feature: Rewatch/reread count is now a synced, ownable field like score or progress, instead of being left out of reconciliation entirely.
- New feature: Rebase mode -- force whichever owner you just set to be true everywhere, retroactively, ignoring the merge base -- joins Mirror/Pull/Merge as a sync mode, and (see above) can also create the show on a connected provider that doesn't have it yet.
- New feature: Synced vs. Platform score display -- a sidebar switch (Qt and GTK) chooses whether the list shows multisync's reconciled Score or the signed-in account's own platform score, with a "Synced Score" column available in both UIs, and a settings toggle to edit an owned score directly in its owner's rating system instead of the signed-in account's. The GTK score slider snaps to the active system's real step (fixing Kitsu's actual quarter/half-star granularity, previously always shown as tenths).
- Improvement: (Qt) The list gains a Release Status column, "Move to" now works from search results for any match (not just ones already in the list), and show details surface the Platform ID.
- Improvement: The Identity tab adds a Type column and right-click Inspect/Open actions, with "open this entry's page" available everywhere an identity issue is shown.
- New feature: Windows packaging -- a PyInstaller-bundled build, cross-built under Wine, so Windows no longer requires a manual Python/GTK/Qt setup to run from source.
- New feature: The title parser falls back through Anitopy before AnimeInfoExtractor when your configured parser's optional dependency isn't installed, and surfaces which fallback is in effect in a dialog instead of silently substituting one.
- New feature: A SubMiner toggle (Qt and GTK) for opening episodes -- reuses an already-running mpv+overlay instance instead of spawning a new one each time.
- Bugfix: (Anitopy parser) Four wrapper bugs fixed, two of which mis-numbered episodes outright.
- New feature: Applying a sync shows a progress bar and log, plus the signed-in account's tracker icon, in the sync window instead of leaving the apply step silent.
- Improvement: Applying bulk pushes now throttles them and retries on a 429 instead of hammering a rate limit into failures, and apply itself is cancellable mid-run, same as fetch already was.
- Bugfix: (MAJOR) The toolbar's Multi-sync entry no longer hangs the whole UI while a sync runs.
- Bugfix: A push failing with 'Apply failed: 'title'' on every single push (a universal crash, not an edge case) is fixed; a use-after-close core dump when the sync window closed mid-apply is fixed; and constructing a provider's library the same way data.py does fixes a MAL 400 and an AniList signal crash on sync startup.
- Bugfix: MAL's integer score rounding was manufacturing spurious conflicts against a decimal local score that hadn't actually changed; score pushes now round half-up and show the rounding rather than treating the rounded value as a real edit.
- Bugfix: (Qt) The engine worker owns starting its thread; previously a call issued at the wrong instant could re-run the just-finished engine call (e.g. re-uploading a whole sync queue) and fire its callback twice.
- Bugfix: Season labels are computed without writing to shared show data; the previous memoization could intermittently abort a cache save from the background MAL-score fetch ("dictionary changed size during iteration").
- Improvement: Multisync treats the signed-in account as the editing surface: changes made in the app (and tracker updates) fold in as your local edits instead of conflicting with the sync database, while field ownership still arbitrates against the other providers. The ownership matrix marks the active account.
- Improvement: Multisync identity resolution auto-links single exact title matches (kept distinguishable from user-confirmed mappings), matches titles across scripts (NFKC-aware; native AniList titles no longer break matching), and accumulates every provider's title variants as entity aliases so romaji/native/English entries find each other. The Identity tab now shows each entry's aliases, year, and why it needs attention.
- Improvement: Multisync identity now links by IDs first and titles last: legacy Kitsu's library download includes Kitsu's own external-site mappings (MAL ids), the community anime-relations database is harvested as a cross-provider id atlas, exact title matches auto-link even when franchise siblings ("Frieren" vs "Frieren Season 2") share a prefix, and franchise siblings from the same provider no longer raise identity questions at all. A Reset database button starts the sync state over cleanly.
- Improvement: Multisync understands differing episode structures for the same work (a 1-episode movie vs a 4-episode listing): completion on one side equals completion on the other, completing a show pushes each provider its own episode total, and partial progress across differing structures surfaces as an explicit conflict instead of a wrong guess.
- Bugfix: Details for legacy-Kitsu shows no longer re-attempt an unsupported details fetch on every request; superseded MAL-score batches persist already-fetched scores; the GTK airing window reports unexpected errors with a traceback instead of swallowing them.
- Improvement: Multisync's "newest wins" arbitration now compares real change times: a remote value's timestamp only advances when the value actually changes, instead of every fetch making the remote side look freshly edited and win every tie against an older local edit.
- Improvement: Entries deleted on a provider's website now drop out of multisync's remote snapshot (and their merge bases) on the next fetch, so the planner stops diffing -- and pushing -- against a phantom entry; local state is never deleted by this, and a re-added entry re-plans as a first sync. An empty list response is deliberately left alone (indistinguishable from a silent API failure).
- Improvement: Multisync fetch isolates per-provider processing failures: a bug ingesting one provider's entries rolls that provider back in one transaction and reports it, instead of aborting the whole fetch with half its rows committed. The single transaction per provider also makes large fetches markedly faster.
- Improvement: The multisync display overlay and planner load their data in bulk instead of issuing per-show queries (an N+1 pattern that ran on the UI thread on every list repaint and after every score edit), and the overlay reuses one database connection instead of reopening and re-migrating the schema each repaint. Title matching during identity resolution is memoized, removing quadratic re-normalization on first syncs of large lists.
- Bugfix: A differing-episode-structures conflict (1-episode movie vs multi-episode listing) no longer offers adopting a provider's raw episode number -- that number is in the provider's own structure and would record a different amount as watched. Both UIs show the provider side as information and offer "Set episodes..." (an explicit count in the local structure) instead, and the engine refuses raw adoption outright.
- Bugfix: An identity conflict whose mapping was quarantined (the type-mismatch guard) reopens even if it had been marked resolved earlier, instead of staying invisibly resolved forever; a user's "decide later" (deferred) status is still preserved across fetches.
- Bugfix: Fields a provider cannot represent (tags on MAL/Kitsu; notes and favorites everywhere, since no backend exposes them yet) are no longer fabricated as empty values in its remote snapshot, and a push the provider could not actually accept is no longer recorded as delivered. The combination could poison the merge base with a value the remote never held, making the provider's real (empty) value read as a fresh remote edit on the next fetch and pull local's value away -- e.g. MAL's inability to store tags eventually erasing tags synced from AniList. Stale fabricated rows are pruned by the next fetch.
- Bugfix: MAL manga rewatch counts used the anime API field name (num_times_rewatched) for both media types; MAL's manga API calls it num_times_reread, so manga rereads always fetched as 0 and pushes were silently ignored -- which multisync would then have recorded as delivered, eventually zeroing the count everywhere.
- Bugfix: Progress merge bases (stored in each provider's own episode structure) are now viewed through the same structure conversion as the remote value when diffing; previously an unchanged completed movie side could read as a phantom 'remote change' against a multi-episode local listing and, in Pull mode, overwrite a deliberate local rewind.
- Bugfix: The 'never push over a field's PROVIDER owner from another source' guard now holds durably: local values remember their provenance, so a value folded in from the signed-in account no longer reaches the owner one sync later disguised as a plain local edit. Direct edits, user resolutions, and the owner itself remain authoritative; Mirror mode still deliberately overrides everyone.
- Bugfix: Multisync's list overlay converts reconciled progress into the active account's own episode structure (no more impossible '4 / 1' cells when one provider lists a movie) and no longer flags values that only differ below the account's own precision (a canonical 8.4 on a 10-point integer account shows a plain 8, not an override cue).
- Bugfix: (GTK) Closing the multi-sync window mid-operation could leak the sync database connection forever due to a thread-liveness race in the deferred close; the worker's completion callback now closes it deterministically.
- Improvement: Fetch & Plan is now cancellable -- closing the sync window no longer freezes the UI until every provider's full list download finishes; the engine stops between providers and entries (worst case: the one in-flight download). A cancelled fetch reports 'Sync cancelled' instead of a bogus failure.
- Improvement: An unresolved identity conflict whose entry is later deleted on the provider's website closes itself ('unlisted') instead of asking about a phantom forever; it reopens normally if the entry is ever re-added. Identity candidate scanning also now snapshots the entity table instead of re-reading and re-parsing it for every unmatched entry, removing quadratic work from first syncs of large lists.
- Improvement: The Qt and GTK sync windows derive the per-media-type database path from one shared definition (hakubun.sync.uibridge.store_path) instead of three copies that could drift onto different files.
- New feature: A "Fetch & plan only" checkbox on the Behavior page (Qt and GTK), on by default while multisync is beta -- Sync fetches and plans in the selected mode, then always opens the sync window for manual review instead of auto-applying clean (non-conflicting, non-first-sync) changes. This replaces the old "Fetch & Plan only" entry in the Mode dropdown, which forced a choice between picking a reconciliation and reviewing it (you could have Merge, or review, but not "review my pulls"); existing configs set to it read back as Merge with the checkbox on, which is what they always meant. Reviewing also now surfaces the window even when the plan turns out to be empty, rather than reporting "already in sync" to the status bar and leaving the window shut.
- New feature: Rebase now also creates the show on any connected tracker that doesn't have it yet -- e.g. a show only on Kitsu, which publishes a MAL id nothing has actually added there -- bundling every owned field's current value into one add. Planned unticked, like a first-sync overwrite: adding a new library entry to a real account is opt-in per show, never applied automatically.
- New feature: After every fetch, multisync asks any provider with an exact reverse-lookup API (AniList's id-by-MAL-id lookup, and Kitsu GraphQL's lookupMapping) whether it has an entity whose MAL id is already known from elsewhere but was never independently matched by title -- e.g. a show only added on Kitsu now also gets linked (and, under Rebase, offered as an add) on AniList, not just MAL, and the reverse for a show only added on MAL/AniList and Kitsu GraphQL.
- Improvement: Rebase's create step now fills in a provider-owned field even when that provider IS the one being created (it has nothing to draw from yet) by falling back to local's current value, instead of leaving the field blank on the new entry.
- Bugfix: (Qt) Switching accounts while a stale async tracker callback (playing/episode-changed) for the PREVIOUS account was still in flight crashed the whole app (KeyError in ShowListModel.update, since setShowList had already rebuilt id_map for the new account); it's now a harmless no-op.
- New feature: (GTK) Added the Parser choice to Settings > Library, at parity with Qt -- GTK previously had no way to select Anitopy or Anitomy-NG at all.
- New feature: Right-click a show and "Set folder..." to manually point it at a specific local folder (Qt and GTK) -- every video file under it is attributed straight to that show (only the episode number is still parsed from the filename), bypassing title-based guessing entirely. The escape hatch for a folder name the parser/guesser can't match. Pinned folders are swept on every library scan, not just when first assigned, and can live outside the configured search directories; "Clear folder" removes the pin.
- Bugfix: (Qt) Resolving one conflict in the multi-sync preview window no longer rebuilds every tab from scratch -- previously that silently unticked every other pending change in the window, discarding review decisions the user hadn't gotten to yet. Only the categories whose changes actually shifted are rebuilt now; everything else, including tick state, is left alone.
- Improvement: Multisync's apply step batches its per-entity mapping/remote-state lookups and writes instead of issuing them one row at a time, and a library scan now saves the library once at the end instead of after every search directory, reusing the signature it already computed rather than re-walking the directory tree a second time to recompute it.
- Improvement: Undo and conflict-resolution-by-id use indexed point lookups instead of scanning the whole event/conflict list, and remote-state writes during a fetch are batched -- both scale with the size of the change, not the size of the library.
- Improvement: (anitomy-ng parser) The parent-folder-title dedup check -- necessary when `library_full_path` is on, since anitomy-ng has no concept of path separators -- no longer runs its (comparatively expensive) search on every file. A cheap precondition now rules out the common case where a match is impossible up front, cutting real-library scan time noticeably with no change in what it matches.

v0.12.2
- New feature: New experimental `anitomy_ng` title parser option (Qt Settings), a pure-Rust filename parser bundled as a required dependency. Faster than the existing Anitopy parser and handles version-numbered titles (e.g. "NieR:Automata Ver1.1a") and season-folder-plus-junk naming patterns more accurately, at the cost of being newer and less battle-tested.
- Bugfix: The default AnimeInfoExtractor parser had no awareness of path separators, so a full relative path (e.g. "Show S01/Show S01E05.mkv") often left a literal "/" and a duplicated title in the extracted show name. Parent/child duplicate-title stripping now runs before parsing.
- Improvement: The "Anitopy" parser option no longer carries an "(Experimental)" label in the Qt Settings dropdown, reflecting its production track record; "Anitomy-NG" carries that label instead as the newer option.
- Legal: Added THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md documenting MPL 2.0 attribution for the anitomy-ng and Anitopy parser dependencies and their upstream origins (Anitomy, anitomy-rs).

v0.12.1
- New feature: Tracker auto-detect in GTK, at parity with Qt -- the "Local" radio was renamed "Auto-detect (recommended)" and now correctly writes `tracker_type: auto` (it previously wrote an unrecognized 'local' value that happened to fall through to the same guessing logic anyway). Auto-detection now also tries MPRIS first before falling back to inotify/polling, since it needs no search directories configured and works with any MPRIS-capable player.
- Improvement: Adjusted several config defaults for a smoother out-of-the-box experience: reuse an existing mpv window; ask to add a show if it's not already in your list; MPRIS updates at 80% of an episode's duration instead of a fixed wait; wait 300 seconds (instead of 120) before updating for local/Jellyfin/Kodi/Plex trackers; download changes automatically on open and send changes after every update and on close; default a newly added show to Plan to Watch instead of the currently active tab; default player set to `/usr/bin/mpv`; default visible columns now include Season and Platform Score.

v0.12
- Rebrand: Hakubun+ is an independent fork of Trackma, adding Taiga mode and an airing schedule window on top of the base Hakubun fork.
- New feature: Optional Kitsu GraphQL backend, selectable in the interface settings. Its library request can retrieve up to 2,000 entries at once, batches detail lookups, and omits synopses from list downloads to reduce transfer time.
- New feature: Cross-reference MyAnimeList community scores for AniList and Kitsu entries, manually or automatically after sync when a MAL account is configured.
- New feature: Undo and redo for progress, score, status, and tag changes in the GTK and Qt interfaces.
- New feature: GTK and Qt now show Season, Type, Platform Score, and MAL Score columns; search results identify shows already in the user's list.
- New feature: GTK list filtering, drag-and-drop moves between status tabs, status-selection confirmation when adding a show, and Ctrl+Z/Ctrl+Shift+Z (or Ctrl+Y) undo/redo shortcuts.
- New feature: Qt toolbar actions, a Move to status menu, improved show-detail layout, and a combined exact score slider/spin control.
- New feature: Taiga Mode (Qt), an optional look that emulates Taiga's UI, including a dedicated Now Playing screen showing what the tracker currently sees playing (title, episode, playback progress, Last/Next Episode buttons) alongside that show's details.
- New feature: Airing Schedule window (GTK and Qt), a week view of airing shows on the user's list cross-referenced from AniList, showing relative air time and whether the user is caught up.
- Improvement: Synopses/descriptions from every backend are sanitized into plain text with real paragraph breaks instead of leaking raw `<br>`/`<p>` markup into the UI.
- Improvement: Kitsu's REST list download writes its information cache once per download rather than once per page, while retaining sequential page loading for correctness.
- Improvement: Scores use appropriate decimal precision; Kitsu's quarter-step 0-5 scale is displayed as a 0-10 half-step scale.
- Improvement: Full library scans are skipped when the watched directories have not changed; automatic retrieval now uses a correct 24-hour day.
- Improvement: Added `HAKUBUN_HOME` to run an isolated Hakubun+ profile without sharing accounts, configuration, cache, or list data with an installed copy.
- Bugfix: Corrected the default `my_last_update` key and refreshed current platform-score/type data from AniList, Kitsu, and MAL.
- Bugfix: (Qt) Fixed a crash on nearly every list action (setting episode/score/status, undo, redo, editing tags, deleting a show, list upload) caused by a worker-callback signature mismatch introduced by the rebrand's worker-dispatch rewrite.
- Bugfix: The library-scanning "whole list" toggle was labeled "Search entire list" in GTK and "Scan through whole list" in Qt for the same setting; both now read "Scan through whole list when matching local files" and explain what it does via tooltip. Added matching tooltips to the status-auto-change, push-sync-on-apply, and per-API-columns settings.
- Bugfix: (GTK) The Kodi tracker's host/port/credential fields existed in the settings window but were never wired up, so they couldn't be configured or saved through the UI at all; Kodi now works the same as Plex and Jellyfin in GTK.
- Improvement: Plex/Jellyfin/Kodi credentials are grouped alphabetically under a single "Streaming Credentials" section at the bottom of the Media settings page (GTK and Qt), instead of being interleaved with the tracker-type picker in an inconsistent order. Their duplicate "use wait time before updating" checkboxes were also consolidated into one shared toggle that follows whichever of Plex/Kodi is currently selected.
- New feature: Optional "Switch to the All category when filtering" toggle (GTK and Qt) that jumps to the All tab when typing in the filter bar, so the search covers the whole list instead of just the current status tab.
- New feature: A "Go to" action for search results already in the user's list -- GTK's Add button becomes "Go to" for such results; Qt gets a "Go to" button in the show-details popup. Both jump to the show's status tab in the main list and highlight it, instead of re-adding it.
- New feature: Right-click "Move to" on search results (GTK and Qt) for results already in the user's list, matching the main list's existing Move to submenu.
- New feature: A manual "Load MAL Scores Now" button in Settings (GTK and Qt), alongside the automatic-after-sync option.

